{
  "title": "Build Part A - Local Invoice Review POC in GitHub Copilot",
  "locked": false,
  "description": "\n\nThe Build section showcases the best innovation from Microsoft for bringing agents to life. Developers love working with our developer solutions. And with GitHub Copilot App, we’re setting the standard for how developer can rapidly build cutting edge agents that deliver real business impact.\n\nOn-Stage Demo Lead: Jessica Deen\n\nBuild Part A - Local Invoice Review POC in GitHub Copilot\n\nWe begin by building a local proof of concept in GitHub Copilot app, demonstrating how the invoice-review agent takes shape, running with local or simulated data, comparing local and cloud execution, and demonstrating sandbox protection before handing off to Foundry and moving to production.",
  "rows": [
    {
      "locked": false,
      "locks": {
        "time": false,
        "narrative": false,
        "demo_actions": false,
        "screenshot": false,
        "visual": false,
        "design_plan": false
      },
      "time": "~35s",
      "narrative": "You heard what Amy needs. I need to get an agent to production fast to make sure our payments are in line with our contracts. But before I promise anyone anything, I want a proof of concept. Something that shows me this can be automated safely and usefully.\n\nI have a lot of choices in where I build, but I start in the GitHub Copilot app. What sets the GitHub Copilot App apart is that it brings agent-driven development natively to GitHub, with the enterprise trust only Microsoft can deliver. It's where I manage the work already in flight, from issues and reviews to my active coding sessions.",
      "demo_actions": "Speak over the Teams visual, then bring the Copilot app forward. Orient the viewer to the home screen and briefly call out sessions, issues, reviews, and active coding sessions.",
      "screenshot": ""
    },
    {
      "locked": false,
      "locks": {
        "time": false,
        "narrative": false,
        "demo_actions": false,
        "screenshot": false,
        "visual": false,
        "design_plan": false
      },
      "time": "~40s",
      "narrative": "Let me prove Caldova's idea. One prompt: 'Help me check our invoices for overbilling,' and the app handles the setup.\n\nFirst, it creates an isolated worktree. Think of it as a safe sandbox where I can experiment without affecting the main branch or anyone else's work.",
      "demo_actions": "Type the prompt into a new session and press Enter. Let the session start, then point at the branch or worktree indicator in the header as setup begins.",
      "screenshot": ""
    },
    {
      "locked": false,
      "locks": {
        "time": false,
        "narrative": false,
        "demo_actions": false,
        "screenshot": false,
        "visual": false,
        "design_plan": false
      },
      "time": "~30s",
      "narrative": "Then it loads a custom skill I built for local agent development. Unlike a one-off prompt, a skill captures reusable instructions and domain knowledge, so every session starts with the same workflow. I can even commit it to the repo so my teammates get the exact same experience.\n\nAnd before anything runs, it checks the local model with a quick preflight.",
      "demo_actions": "Stay in the setup flow. Point at the skill-load line for the local-agent skill, then let the local model preflight run and land on the phi4-mini checkmark.",
      "screenshot": null
    },
    {
      "locked": false,
      "locks": {
        "time": false,
        "narrative": false,
        "demo_actions": false,
        "screenshot": false,
        "visual": false,
        "design_plan": false
      },
      "time": "~35s",
      "narrative": "Here's the app. On the left, the Copilot session driving the work. On the right, the Canvas - a live, interactive view of the agent I'm building.\n\nThis is the part to watch. I'm not just chatting with an agent. I can click right inside the Canvas - here, I'll add the Aster Ridge invoice - and the Canvas gives the agent real context to work with. Think of it as a modern, shareable version of localhost development, something I can check into a repo or hand to a teammate.",
      "demo_actions": "Show the Local Agent canvas with the Anatomy drawer expanded. Gesture left to right from the Copilot session to the Canvas, then click 'Add the Aster Ridge invoice (PDF)' so the PDF attaches and the context tile lights up.",
      "screenshot": ""
    },
    {
      "locked": false,
      "locks": {
        "time": false,
        "narrative": false,
        "demo_actions": false,
        "screenshot": false,
        "visual": false,
        "design_plan": false
      },
      "time": "~30s",
      "narrative": "And to be clear, this isn't just a model. It's an agent: a model, plus instructions, plus context. Right now it's running on my laptop using a local model. That means lower latency, the ability to work offline, and the assurance that my data never leaves my machine. Even on an airplane, I can keep building and testing.",
      "demo_actions": "Point at the expanded Anatomy drawer to show Model, Instructions, Context, and Memory. Collapse it briefly if helpful so the icons stay visible while you call out local execution.",
      "screenshot": ""
    },
    {
      "locked": false,
      "locks": {
        "time": false,
        "narrative": false,
        "demo_actions": false,
        "screenshot": false,
        "visual": false,
        "design_plan": false
      },
      "time": "~40s",
      "narrative": "Now that it has the invoice, I can trigger the audit from the Canvas itself.\n\nThe agent immediately gets to work, entirely locally. It checks the invoice math and catches a line that doesn't add up: line 2 bills $552,720, but quantity times rate is $546,000. That's a $6,720 overstatement it can prove from the invoice alone.\n\nThat's the first proof. The workflow works locally, and it also shows me the boundary. It can prove math issues from the invoice itself, but negotiated rates and packaging authorization need the contract.",
      "demo_actions": "Click the 'Audit locally' chip, or ask the audit question from the Canvas. Show the local agent streaming findings and pause on the line 2 math issue and the deferred contract questions.",
      "screenshot": ""
    },
    {
      "locked": false,
      "locks": {
        "time": false,
        "narrative": false,
        "demo_actions": false,
        "screenshot": false,
        "visual": false,
        "design_plan": false
      },
      "time": "~35s",
      "narrative": "Before I take this any further, I want a second opinion. So I ask Copilot to rubber duck the results. In plain English, that means I have it review its own work from a skeptical angle and look for anything it may have overstated or missed.\n\nIt's a simple way to pressure-test the findings before I move on. I want to confirm what the local agent can support, and just as important, where it should stop until it has the contract.",
      "demo_actions": "Type '/rubberduck the invoice findings - where could they be overclaiming?' and start the review.",
      "screenshot": ""
    },
    {
      "locked": false,
      "locks": {
        "time": false,
        "narrative": false,
        "demo_actions": false,
        "screenshot": false,
        "visual": false,
        "design_plan": false
      },
      "time": "~30s",
      "narrative": "That critique may take a minute, and that's okay. I'm on a plane; the point of this PoC was to prove the workflow works locally and to learn exactly what the local agent cannot know without the contract. Once I'm back in the office and the rubber duck has finished, I'll be ready to move the same agent into Microsoft Foundry - production-ready, with the contracts it needs.",
      "demo_actions": "Tee up the pivot while rubber duck runs. Keep the rubber-duck review visible and queue the Foundry move for Part 2.",
      "screenshot": null
    }
